--- MESSAGE ---
Emission is the super-domain's product, and the receiver matches it

Last night's refinement of transforms — composition by concatenation and substitution, substitution
strings as target-domain types, per-aspect forms, and RULES THAT ARE TARGET-LANGUAGE-NEUTRAL — put
three freshly landed pieces out of alignment. This is the first of the three, and the smallest: the
super-domain gets a product type that names no target, and the receiver matches that.

WHAT MOVED

    draco.generator.Emission          from Primal(String); par value String        (was gendrake, from Surface)
    draco.generator.EmissionReceived  var emission Emission -> received.add        (was SurfaceReceived)
    draco.gendrake.Emit               fix emission draco.generator.Emission parameters
                                        par [ GenDrake.generator parameters par definition ]
    draco.gendrake.Emitter            par received java.util.List(draco.generator.Emission)

Emission was Potency's shape — derives the target's type, takes a parameter typed in the source, body
calls Drake.emit. Every one of those three is a target reference, and the receiver in the super-domain
could only match it by naming draketarget.Surface. Now it is Surface's shape — Compound's — one level
up: the rendered text of a definition, whatever the target rendered it. It takes the text; it does not
know how the text was made. The knowing moved to Emit, which is in gendrake and may know: it applies
the domain's own `generator` global, which has been `draco.Drake.emit` since 1cb3578, and wraps the
result. That is the first bracketed nested argument in src/main, and DrakeCLI parse carries it back as
the tree it was authored as.

Surface stays in draketarget as the target's own type. Nothing produces it today; when a GenDrake
product needs to be drake-typed and not merely text, it is there to derive.

WHAT DID NOT

The gate. GenDrakeTest is the same two assertions with the names moved: the Emitter's Knowledge
accepts Emit and EmissionReceived, and inserting every src/main definition and firing yields the set
of committed .drake files — 91 of 91. The oracle is still the files. DracoGenerator is untouched; the
domain member counts net to zero (one type left gendrake, one arrived in generator), so the parse
scope stays 93 and the suite stays 592 / 42.

Emit still lives in gendrake, and Generator(T) still has no `generate`. Those are the other two
realignments, each its own commit.

